\pard \fs24
{\plain \fs36 \i\f1\fs36 Concerning the Service}{\plain \fs24 \f1 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f1 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 In the Daily Office, the term "Officiant" is used to denote the person,\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 clerical or lay, who leads the Office.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 It is appropriate that other persons be assigned to read the Lessons, and \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 to lead other parts of the service not assigned to the officiant. The bishop, \softline
when present, appropriately concludes the Office with a blessing.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 At celebrations of the Holy Eucharist, the Order for Morning or Evening\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 Prayer may be used in place of all that precedes the Offertory.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f1\fs20 Additional Directions are on page 141.\par
}{\plain \fs24 \f1 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f1 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f1 \par
}\column 
